<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emmanuel Philibert, Duke of Savoy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1528">1528</a>–<a href="/years/1580">1580</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Emmanuel Philibert of Savoy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emmanuel Philibert, Duke of Savoy, won fame as a general for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, defeating the French at St Quentin in 1557. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-cateau-cambresis">Peace of Cateau-Cambrésis</a> in 1559 restored his duchy, occupied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and he moved the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chambery">Chambéry</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>. He reorganised the state's finances and army, laying the foundations of later Savoyard power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emmanuel-philibert-duke-of-savoy.jpg" alt="Emmanuel Philibert, Duke of Savoy" width="320" height="521" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paolo Veronese · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emanuele_Filiberto_di_Savoia_detto_il_TESTA_DI_FERRO.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
